t W5 and W8 are available (W5:3, W8:4). T8 alone, could pair or chow with T6/T7/T9, but T9 scarce. B2 pair (I have two B2), could make pung.\n\nBetter to keep pairs or near sequences. I have:\n- T8: two copies (pair), good for pair or pung.\n- B2: two copies (pair), good.\n- W1: single terminal.\n- B1: single terminal.\n- F2: single wind.\n- F4: single wind (but seat wind, potential for Seat Wind pattern if pung).\n- J3: single dragon.\n- W6, W7: singles, could form chow with W5/W8.\n\nFor efficiency, keep pairs and potential chows. Discard singles that are less useful.\n\nOption 1: Discard W1 (terminal, but single, no immediate use). \nOption 2: Discard F4 (wind, but seat wind, could be useful for pung if I draw more; but only 2 left unshown).\nOption 3: Discard J3 (dragon, single).\nOption 4: Discard B1 (terminal, single).\nOption 5: Discard F2 (wind, single).\nOption 6: Discard T8 (pair, but pair is valuable; discarding breaks pair).\nOption 7: Discard W6 or W7 (simples, could form chow).\n\nSince I need a pair for winning hand, keeping pairs (T8, B2) is important. I have two pairs: T8 and B2. I might use one as pair and try to pung the other.\n\nFor Outside Hand, I need all melds to contain terminals/honors. My current meld W9 qualifies. If I keep terminals/honors, I should discard simples first: W6, W7 are simples. But W6 and W7 could combine with W5/W8 to form chow, but chows of simples would not qualify for Outside Hand unless they contain terminals? Outside Hand requires every meld and pair to have terminals or honors. So a chow of simples (e.g., W5-W6-W7) does NOT qualify. Therefore, for Outside Hand, I cannot have any meld of all simples. I must form pungs/kongs of terminals/honors, or chows that include terminals (like 1-2-3 or 7-8-9). So keeping W6, W7 is not helpful for Outside Hand.\n\nThus, I should discard W6 or W7 first. Between them, W7 has 4 unshown, W6 has 3 unshown. Slightly more W7 available. But discard either.\n\nAlso, B2 is simple, so for Ou